Redesign the entire user experience of my website, "The Pressure Paradox," around a clear user journey so visitors never feel overwhelmed.

Main Goal:
The website should help students understand how external pressure affects their decisions, stress levels, and career choices, then guide them toward personalized resources based on their specific challenges.

Design Principles:

* Minimal text.
* One idea per screen.
* Clean, modern, student-friendly design.
* Plenty of whitespace.
* Large headings and short descriptions.
* Avoid information overload.
* Every section should naturally lead to the next section.
* Focus on user experience over displaying large amounts of information.

WEBSITE FLOW:

SECTION 1: HERO (HOME PAGE)

Large headline:
"The Pressure Paradox"

One-sentence explanation:
"Helping students understand how pressure shapes their choices, stress, and future."

Primary button:
"Start Your Journey"

Do NOT include large paragraphs.

SECTION 2: WHY THIS MATTERS

Immediately show research statistics to establish credibility.

Use visual cards, charts, and infographics instead of long text.

Examples:

* Percentage of students experiencing academic stress.
* Percentage of students feeling pressure about future careers.
* Percentage of students reporting burnout.

Each statistic should be displayed in a clean, visually engaging card.

Add a short section title:
"You are not alone."

SECTION 3: STUDENT STORIES

Place student stories immediately after the statistics.

Purpose:
Help visitors emotionally connect with the issue before taking the quiz.

Use 3-5 short testimonial cards.

Each story should be only 1-2 sentences.

Examples:

* Academic pressure
* Family expectations
* Career uncertainty
* Burnout

Use a carousel or card layout.

SECTION 4: PERSONALIZED PRESSURE QUIZ

This is the centerpiece of the website.

Create a guided assessment with approximately 5 questions.

The quiz should identify which type of pressure affects the student most.

Possible categories:

1. Academic Stress
2. Burnout & Mental Health
3. Time Management & Study Skills
4. Career & Major Uncertainty
5. Fear of Judgment
6. Family Expectations
7. Unconventional Career Interests

The quiz should calculate scores behind the scenes and determine the user's dominant pressure category.

After submission, automatically route users to a personalized results page.

RESULT PATHWAYS:

If Academic Stress is highest:
→ Direct to Study Tools page.

If Burnout, Anxiety, or Emotional Stress is highest:
→ Direct to Mental Health Resources page.

If Family Pressure or Personal Challenges are highest:
→ Direct to School Counselor Support page.

If Career Uncertainty or Unconventional Career Interests are highest:
→ Direct to Exploring Your Own Path page.

PERSONALIZED RESULTS PAGE

Display:

* Their primary pressure category.
* A short explanation.
* Reassurance that many students experience similar challenges.
* Personalized recommendations.
* Button to access recommended resources.

RESOURCE PAGES

Keep all resource pages concise.

Use cards, icons, accordions, checklists, and interactive elements.

Avoid large walls of text.

PAGE: Study Tools

* Time management
* Active recall
* Spaced repetition
* Pomodoro technique
* Exam preparation

PAGE: Mental Health Resources

* Burnout signs
* Stress management
* Self-care habits
* Mindfulness
* Seeking support

PAGE: School Counselor Support

* When to seek help
* Academic planning
* College planning
* Talking with trusted adults
* Frequently asked questions

PAGE: Exploring Your Own Path

Theme:
"Success Doesn't Have One Definition."

Include:

* Challenging traditional definitions of success.
* How pressure can influence career decisions.
* Examples of unconventional careers.
* Career exploration activities.
* Reflection prompts.
* Myths vs Facts section.
* Stories of people who pursued unique career paths.

FINAL SECTION

After every resource page, include:

"Your Next Step"

A single action item:

* Talk to a counselor.
* Try a study strategy.
* Reflect on career interests.
* Create a stress-management plan.

END GOAL

The entire website should feel like a guided experience:

1. Learn
2. Relate
3. Reflect
4. Discover your pressure type
5. Receive personalized guidance
6. Take action

Build the site using modern React and Tailwind CSS.

Use smooth transitions, subtle animations, responsive layouts, modern cards, and a clean visual hierarchy similar to high-quality educational or wellness platforms.

Prioritize clarity, simplicity, and user guidance over displaying large amounts of information.
